<PLUGIN name="&name;" author="&author;" version="&version;" launch="&launch;"
        pluginURL="&pluginURL;" min="6.10.0"
        icon="exchange"
        project="https://github.com/TZERO78/buddynet"
        support="https://github.com/TZERO78/buddynet/issues"
        readme="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/TZERO78/buddynet/main/unraid/BuddyNet/README.md">

<CHANGES>
**BuddyNet** — one binary, three roles. On Unraid you run the `buddy` role: a
zero-config, end-to-end-encrypted tunnel to your buddy. It finds your partner via
a small handshake server, hole-punches through NAT (no port forwarding), and
falls back to a blind relay only if a direct path is impossible. Point
rsync/borg/kopia at the local socket and back up directly between two sites — in
either direction over one tunnel. Two buddies is the simplest case; with a
manifest, one node can hold many at once (MultiPeer).

###2026.09.04
- **Binary v5.5.0** — the 2026-09-04 hardening release. Nothing changes on this
  page. What changes underneath: a stranger probing the handshake port gets
  uniform refusals and no product name; the buddy now checks every field of the
  roster the server signs (id, name, endpoints) instead of trusting them; and the
  relay it falls back to must be on the same host as the handshake server (the
  usual VPS setup) — a relay elsewhere is skipped and logged, and would need the
  `--peer-relay` flag, which this page does not expose. Full list in the
  project CHANGELOG.

###2026.08.26.2
- **Direct mode is now available on this page.** The new *Mode* selector chooses
  between **Coordinator** (the usual setup, and what your install keeps doing —
  nothing changes unless you switch) and **Direct**, which needs no handshake
  server at all.
- In Direct mode you fill in your buddy's address (*Buddy endpoint*, a DynDNS
  name is fine — it is re-resolved on every attempt) and/or a *Listen port* so
  your buddy can dial this server. At least one of the two is needed; if only one
  of you is reachable, that side sets the port and the other sets the endpoint.
- **The Buddy key is required in Direct mode.** Without a handshake server there
  is no invite and no safety-code step, so the pinned key is the only thing that
  authenticates your buddy. The service refuses to start without it, and says so
  in the log, rather than coming up unauthenticated.
- The price of Direct mode is router work: the reachable side needs its UDP port
  forwarded to this server. Coordinator mode still needs nothing from your router.
- A leftover invite is ignored in Direct mode (there is no server to redeem it
  at) and this is noted in the log, rather than being passed to the daemon, which
  would refuse the combination.
- No new binary: still pinned to buddynet **v5.4.0**.

###2026.08.26.1
- Pinned to buddynet **v5.4.0** — two ways to run BuddyNet without renting a
  server, neither of which changes anything about how the plugin works today.
- **The coordinator may be one of the two buddies.** `--role=buddy,handshake,relay`
  always worked; nothing said so. It is now documented with the conditions it
  really needs: the router in front of it must allow NAT loopback (hairpinning)
  and forward both UDP ports, or it does not work at all.
- **`--direct` needs no coordinator whatsoever.** Each side is told where its
  buddy is (a DynDNS name is fine — it is re-resolved on every attempt) and pins
  its key, and that is the whole configuration. Command line only for now: the
  plugin's own page has no direct-mode control, so nothing here changes.
- Nothing about pairing or the protocol changes (protocol version stays 8), and a
  v5.4.0 buddy works with one still on v5.3.3.
- Checksum gate set to the v5.4.0 `buddynet-linux-amd64` sha256, verified against
  the published artifact (sha256 + cosign + SLSA provenance) before pinning.

###2026.08.26
- Pinned to buddynet **v5.3.3** — a hardening fix on the buddy's own tunnel
  socket. It now validates the source address of every incoming QUIC connection
  (the control plane has done this since v4.1.1) and closes its listener as soon
  as the tunnel is up, since a buddy only ever has one partner. Before this, the
  port kept answering strangers for as long as the tunnel was open, and each
  attempt cost a full TLS handshake.
- What this is NOT: nobody could read your tunnel or get into it either way —
  the partner is pinned by key. What an outsider could do was make the process
  burn CPU and memory. Nothing about pairing or the protocol changes, and a
  v5.3.3 buddy works with one still on v5.3.2.
- Checksum gate set to the v5.3.3 `buddynet-linux-amd64` sha256, taken from the
  published artifact after downloading it and re-computing the hash — and
  cross-checked against the release's cosign signature and SLSA attestation.

###2026.08.25.2
- Pinned to buddynet **v5.3.2**. The binary is unchanged in behaviour since
  v5.2.1 (no protocol change, no new flags); v5.3.0–v5.3.2 are an external-audit
  round plus documentation and firewall-template fixes.
- Checksum gate set to the v5.3.2 `buddynet-linux-amd64` sha256, taken from the
  published artifact after downloading it and re-computing the hash — and
  cross-checked against the release's cosign signature and SLSA attestation.
- The firewall templates under `deployments/` only matter if you run your own
  handshake/relay VPS — the Unraid buddy role here does not use them. For the
  record: v5.3.1 fixed an `iptables.rules` that could not be loaded at all, and
  v5.3.2 corrects that file's IPv6 `--hashlimit-srcmask` guidance. The shipped
  rule value is unchanged in both; only comments and the recommendation changed.

###2026.08.24
- Pinned to buddynet **v5.2.1** — the documentation-integrity release from an
  external audit. The binaries are unchanged in behaviour (no protocol change, no
  new flags); what changed is what the documentation claims and how the release is
  built and attested.
- Checksum gate set to the v5.2.1 `buddynet-linux-amd64` sha256, taken from the
  published artifact after downloading it and re-computing the hash, not from the
  build log.
- Of note for anyone running their own handshake server: approval mode
  (`--authorized`) is now the documented recommendation for a private server, and
  the VPS firewall example in the project docs was corrected — the shipped
  `deployments/nftables.conf` was always right, the guide's copy of it was not.
- buddynet v5.2.1 is built with go1.26.7 and its release artifacts now carry a
  SLSA build provenance attestation in addition to the cosign signature.

###2026.08.20.5
- **The plugin can create an invite now.** Until today it could only *join* one,
  so pairing two Unraid servers meant dropping into a shell on one of them. The
  *Invite* row has a **create invite** button: it mints a fresh rendezvous token,
  puts that in your own field, and shows the full `bnet1.…` string to send to
  your buddy. That string carries your identity, so their side pins **you**
  automatically — nothing for them to type into their Buddy key field.
- **First contact is four steps and neither side needs a terminal** — see the new
  section on the settings page and in the plugin README. Nobody has to be online
  at the same time and nobody types a code: your buddy sends their identity back
  and you paste it into *Buddy key*.
- **Your identity now comes from the binary instead of being grepped out of the
  log**, so the page shows it on a fresh install and after a log rotation — which
  is exactly when you need it for pairing.
- Binary unchanged (still **v5.2.0** — this is a settings-page change only).

###2026.08.20.4
- Wording only — **the binary is unchanged (still v5.1.1)**, nothing to re-download
  and nothing about your existing setup changes.
- **Corrected the claims about the Buddy key that stopped being true in v5.1.0.**
  The settings page, this plugin's README and the service log all said you *must*
  set the **Buddy key** or the connection is refused. That only ever applied to a
  **bare token**. A `bnet1.…` invite carries your buddy's identity and BuddyNet
  pins it from there — no Buddy key needed. All three now say which case is which,
  and the service logs a NOTE instead of a WARNING when you paste a key-bearing
  invite without a Buddy key.
- The *Token* wording on the settings page now consistently says **Invite**,
  matching the field names.
- The invite is now handed to the daemon through the environment instead of the
  command line, so it no longer shows up in `ps` for other processes on the
  server.

###2026.08.20.3
- Pinned to buddynet **v5.2.0** — the hardening release from the 2026-08-20
  audit. **Two behaviour changes worth knowing:**
  - The **Buddy key** is now checked on *every* connect. If it names a different
    key than the one stored from an earlier pairing, the tunnel does not come up
    and the log says so. That is a re-pin or a revocation: press **Forget buddy**,
    then pair again with a NEW invite.
  - **Forget buddy** is now a real revocation. Before, it only wiped the trust
    store while the invite and the Buddy key survived in the plugin config, so the
    same buddy re-paired within seconds. It now records the key on this node's
    revocation list and leaves the service stopped. **Allow buddy again** lifts
    it, and refuses unless a Buddy key *and* an invite are configured.
- Checksum gate set to the v5.2.0 `buddynet-linux-amd64` sha256 (downloaded from
  the release and verified locally, together with its cosign signature).
- Settings page: the SMB section is named for what it does instead of carrying a
  product name. No behaviour change — same fields, same config keys.

###2026.08.19.1
- Pinned to buddynet **v5.1.1** — a security fix for the **relay** role. **Update
  your relay**; a buddy-only Unraid install is not affected on its own, but the
  relay it uses should be on v5.1.1.
  - The relay's address-validation cookie did not cover the source **port**, so
    someone on the same public IP as one of the buddies (same NAT or LAN) who had
    captured a valid relay bind could replay it from another port, and the relay
    would treat it as that buddy moving and send its traffic to them instead.
  - **Your data stays private either way** — the tunnel is encrypted end to end
    and the relay never holds a key to it. The damage is a dropped connection and
    the fact that someone learns a session exists.
  - Also: the relay's "one source holds the max legs" warning no longer writes
    the source address into a normal log (only with the debug flag), and the
    security documentation was brought back in line with what v5 actually speaks.

###2026.08.19
- Pinned to buddynet **v5.1.0** — a security improvement to pairing, **no
  coordinated upgrade needed**: nothing on the wire changed, so a v5.1 node pairs
  with a v5.0 one as before.
  - **The invite your buddy gives you now carries their identity.** It looks like
    `bnet1.&lt;token&gt;.&lt;key&gt;` instead of a bare token. Paste the **whole** string into
    the *Invite* field (or the invite file) — BuddyNet pins your buddy's key
    straight from it, so a compromised handshake server cannot slip a different
    machine in on the other end. Nothing for you to compare, and it works
    headless.
  - An older bare token still works exactly as before. A **truncated or edited**
    invite is refused rather than quietly pairing with less protection — if you
    see that error, copy the invite again in full.
  - Your buddy (the inviting side) is asked once for the six-character code shown
    on **your** screen, and is deliberately shown no code of their own, so it can
    only come from a phone call. That side needs a terminal; an unattended
    inviter should pin with a buddy key instead.

###2026.08.13.2
- Pinned to buddynet **v5.0.0** — a major release. **⚠ Coordinated upgrade
  required (protocol 7→8):** the handshake server **and every buddy** must be on
  v5.0.0; a v4 node will not pair with a v5 one in either direction, and there is
  deliberately no compatibility fallback. Either update both sides together, or
  run the old and new server side by side on two ports and move pair by pair.
  - **Removed settings:** *Encrypt the control plane* is gone — matchmaking is
    always encrypted now, so there is nothing left to switch. A fixed pairing
    token is gone too: pairing is one-time (invite/join) and retires itself.
  - **If you run a relay on a VPS:** it now refuses to start without an
    authorization policy. Give it `--server-key SERVER_KEY --relay-id RELAY_ID`
    (verify tickets your handshake server signs — recommended) and/or
    `--allow-cidr`. This does not affect a buddy-only Unraid install.
  - **If your server key file ever goes missing, the server now refuses to
    start** instead of quietly becoming a *different* server that every buddy
    rejects. Create it once with `buddynet --key PATH init`.
  - New: relay tickets with proof of possession, a QUIC-only control plane, a
    control server that keeps no runtime state, and the v5 audit fixes.

###2026.08.13
- Pinned to buddynet **v4.1.1** — a security release, **no settings-page and no
  protocol change** (still protocol 7; no re-pairing, no coordinated upgrade).
  - **Fixes an unauthenticated denial of service against a public relay.** The
    relay counted its abuse budgets per exact source address while the control
    plane already counted IPv6 per /64, so a single /64 — which one site gets for
    free — could mint unlimited "distinct sources" and fill the relay's session
    table, locking everyone else out. All per-source budgets now share one rule
    (IPv4 per address, IPv6 per /64). A refused bind no longer costs a session
    slot either.
  - Only relevant if you run a **relay**; a buddy-only install is unaffected but
    should update anyway.
  - This bump also skips past v4.1.0, which the plugin never pinned.

###2026.08.11
- Pinned to buddynet **v4.0.0** — a control-plane security release.
  - **⚠ BREAKING control-plane change (protocol 6→7):** the handshake server
    **and every buddy** must be on v4.0.0 — older nodes will not pair, in either
    direction. There is deliberately no compatibility fallback: a v3 registration
    proof does not cover the fields v4 relies on, so honouring it would mean
    accepting a weaker proof. If your buddy is not upgrading at the same time,
    either coordinate the update or run the old and new servers side by side on
    two ports and migrate pair by pair (see `docs/PROTOCOL.md`). No settings-page
    change and no re-pairing needed once both sides are on v4.0.0.
  - Fixes that approval mode (`--authorized`) never worked end to end over the
    default QUIC transport: an un-approved client could not deliver its `--code`,
    so the operator could never approve it. Enrollment now completes without a
    restart on either side.
  - Registrations carry a per-attempt nonce and a signature covering every field
    the server acts on; the server derives the virtual IP from the key instead of
    trusting the claim; the address-validation cookie now gates every expensive
    operation; control connections are bounded per source and in time; a peer
    reaches `peers.json` only after its identity is confirmed; and a deleted
    allowlist now means zero authorized clients instead of silently keeping the
    last-loaded keys.
  - Adds a second binary, `buddynet-handshake`, for anyone running a public
    matchmaker — not used by this plugin, which continues to ship the full
    `buddynet`. Checksum gate set to the v4.0.0 `buddynet-linux-amd64` sha256.

###2026.07.04.2
- Pinned to buddynet **v3.0.1** — a hardening + documentation release, **no
  settings-page or protocol change** (plugin behaviour unchanged; no re-pairing).
  Crypto core: added known-answer/interop and verification-path tamper tests, a
  key-load TOCTOU fix (symlink-safe `O_NOFOLLOW` open) and best-effort in-memory
  wiping of derived secrets; the shared identity-key reuse is now documented as a
  reviewed, guarded design. `SECURITY.md` was rewritten threat-model-first and a
  user-friendly "run your own VPS coordinator" HowTo added. Release binaries are
  now Linux amd64/arm64 only (BuddyNet is a Linux tool). Checksum gate set to the
  v3.0.1 `buddynet-linux-amd64` sha256.

###2026.07.04.1
- **BuddyShare section** on the settings page — share folders with your buddy
  over SMB, scoped: the buddy reaches ONLY Samba (:445) over the tunnel, and
  inside Samba only the shares you grant. Clean separation of duties: the
  plugin configures **only the tunnel scope** (enabling BuddyShare visibly
  fills in WireGuard + port 445); **users, shares and permissions stay 100% in
  Unraid's own UI** — this plugin never creates or changes users. The section
  warns prominently about **Public shares** (visible to any SMB client, so
  also to the buddy), checks whether the named share user exists, and shows
  the exact server/user info the buddy enters on the other side (soft-mount
  hint included). E2e lab-proven pattern (`lab/test-buddyshare.sh`, 8/8).
- The BuddyShare section is **bilingual**: with a German language pack
  installed and active it renders in German (own dictionary via the webGUI's
  plugin-translation mechanism), otherwise English. No binary change (v3.0.0).

###2026.07.04
- Pinned to buddynet **v3.0.0** — the Phase 3 release. The **WireGuard data
  plane** (`--wireguard`) and **scoped exposure** (`--expose`) are now released:
  the settings page's "WireGuard data plane" and "Exposed ports" fields now work
  — with a **secure default**: with WireGuard enabled and no Exposed ports set,
  your buddy reaches **NOTHING** on this server (fail-closed). Name a port
  (e.g. `873`) to share exactly that, or `all` for explicit whole-host access.
  Enforced in the kernel (own nftables table over raw netlink) — no
  nft/iptables/ufw/firewalld needed, an existing firewall setup is not touched.
  - **Breaking (control plane, both sides must match):** v3.0.0 encrypts the
    matchmaking by default. The plugin already passes this flag explicitly per
    its "Encrypt the control plane" setting, so plugin behaviour is unchanged —
    but your **handshake server must also be on v3.0.0** (or both sides opt
    into the legacy plain-UDP mode).
  - First-contact safety check (SAS) is now **typed in** rather than
    y/N-confirmed — does not affect this plugin (headless runs pinned via the
    Buddy key); noted for CLI users.
  - Checksum gate set to the v3.0.0 `buddynet-linux-amd64` sha256.

###2026.06.20.3
- Pinned to buddynet **v2.3.0**. Adds a **hard limit of 48 buddies per node** (a
  deliberate design limit for a personal overlay — over-limit manifests are
  refused fail-closed, never silently truncated) plus VIP-collision rejection at
  peer assembly. Hardening: the key file is **refused if it is a symlink** (no
  follow on stat/chmod/write). Relay abuse ceilings are now configurable
  (`--relay-max-sessions` / `--relay-max-legs-per-ip`). The lab-only `--insecure`
  flag was renamed to `--lab` (the plugin never used it; production is unaffected).
  No settings-page or protocol change from v2.2.1. Checksum gate set to the v2.3.0
  `buddynet-linux-amd64` sha256.

###2026.06.20.2
- Pinned to buddynet **v2.2.1** — bug-fix release. Fixes a **re-pair deadlock on
  one-sided session loss** (a one-sided restore-from-backup, or a `peers remove` +
  `peers add` re-invite, no longer strands a buddy): a stale session now falls
  back to the manifest bootstrap token to recover, key stays pinned. Also a
  readable `peers list` and the connection lifecycle in the log schema. No
  settings-page or protocol change from v2.2.0. Checksum gate set to the v2.2.1
  `buddynet-linux-amd64` sha256.

###2026.06.20.1
- Pinned to buddynet **v2.2.0** — security-hardening release. The pairing token
  is now **sealed to the server's key** (no cleartext token on plain UDP), plus
  panic isolation, a lock-free relay, bounded `-forward` streams, enforced key
  perms, an env-gated `--insecure`, and startup VIP reconcile. Checksum gate set
  to the v2.2.0 `buddynet-linux-amd64` sha256.
  - **Breaking control-plane change (protocol 5→6):** the handshake server and
    every buddy must be on v2.2.0 — older nodes will not pair. No data-plane or
    settings-page change.

###2026.06.20
- Pinned to buddynet **v2.1.0** — the binary gains **MultiPeer**: one node can
  hold many buddy tunnels at once (`--peers-file` manifest, `--vip-listen`
  name routing, `buddynet peers list/add/remove`, live reload on `SIGHUP`); one
  failing buddy never affects the others. Checksum gate set to the v2.1.0
  `buddynet-linux-amd64` sha256.
  - The Unraid settings page still drives the single-buddy setup; MultiPeer is
    available from the command line for now. No breaking change — additive on
    the v2.0.0 wire format and virtual IPs.

###2026.06.19.2
- Renamed the `.buddy` name-resolution feature to **BuddyDNS** (settings-page
  labels + docs only; no functional change, same `--name`/`--dns` flags).

###2026.06.19.1
- Pinned to buddynet **v2.0.0** (first release carrying BuddyDNS/Lazy *and* the
  `/16` virtual-IP change). Checksum gate set to the v2.0.0 `buddynet-linux-amd64`
  sha256.
  - **BREAKING:** v2.0.0 widens the virtual IP to `10.66.X.Y` (a `/16`), so every
    node's virtual IP changes — your buddy must **re-pin** your Buddy key after
    the update.

###2026.06.19
- Surfaced **BuddyDNS** and the **Lazy tunnel** on the settings page:
  - **BuddyDNS name** (`--name`) — reachable as `&lt;name&gt;.buddy`.
  - **BuddyDNS resolver** (`--dns`) — answer `*.buddy` on `127.0.0.153:53`
    (runs as root here, so the privileged bind succeeds).
  - **Lazy tunnel** (`--lazy`) — defer the encrypted tunnel until the first `-L`
    connection arrives; ignored (with a log warning) when no `-L` is set.
- MAINTAINER: bump `BINVER`/`BINSHA` to the **v2.0.0** release (the first build
  carrying these features and the `/16` virtual-IP change) before publishing.

###2026.06.15.1
- Pinned to buddynet **v1.2.1** — completes the structured log schema: a
  virtual-IP mismatch now logs `SECURITY: event=vip-mismatch` at the detection
  point (all detection points now emit `SECURITY:` events). Checksum gate bumped
  to the v1.2.1 `buddynet-linux-amd64` sha256.

###2026.06.15
- Pinned to buddynet **v1.2.0** (security hardening + structured logging release):
  - Relay return-routability cookie, open-mode proof-of-possession, and
    token-squat / intrusion detection on the handshake server.
  - Unified, security-aware log schema (`SECURITY:` / state transitions /
    per-minute stats with `ALERT`). The buddy now tags its journal lines with
    `SyslogIdentifier=buddynet-buddy@NAME` for per-tunnel filtering.
  - Clock-skew diagnostics, plaintext-token and revocation warnings.
- Binary checksum gate bumped to the v1.2.0 `buddynet-linux-amd64` sha256.

###2026.06.14.1
- Aligned with the buddy SAS / session model:
  - Runs `--no-interactive` (headless has no terminal to compare a first-contact
    SAS), so an unknown buddy key is refused, never learned blind.
  - The Buddy key (pin) is now effectively required — the page and service warn
    when it is missing, since the connection is refused without it.
  - Uses the legacy fixed-token mode (token + pinned buddy key), the right
    pattern for an unattended node; identity key, trust store and peer cache stay
    on the array.

###2026.06.14
- Initial BuddyNet Unraid client plugin, ported from the hardware-tested
  BuddyPeer plugin:
  - Settings page (Tools -&gt; BuddyNet) + boot/array-managed service running
    `buddynet --role=buddy`.
  - Bidirectional over one tunnel: -L (push to buddy) and -forward (receive).
  - Live status: buddy traffic-light, per-direction transfer + throughput.
  - Dashboard tile with a buddy online/offline traffic-light.
  - Binary verified against a pinned SHA256 on every install.
  - Secrets kept off the FAT flash: token file and the identity key + trust
    store + peer cache live on /mnt/user/appdata/buddynet/ (real 0600).
  - Danger zone: forget buddy = real revocation (the key goes on the revocation
    list, so it cannot re-pair with the saved invite), allow buddy again, reset
    identity (shred where supported).
  - Clean uninstall removes config, identity, trust store and peer cache.

> **Maintainer note:** before publishing, set BINVER/BINURL/BINSHA in the
> install section below to a real `buddynet` release asset
> (`buddynet-linux-amd64`) and its sha256. Until then the checksum gate will
> (correctly) refuse to install.
</CHANGES>

<!-- webGUI settings page (Tools -> BuddyNet) -->
<FILE Name="&emhttp;/buddynet.page">
<INLINE>
<![CDATA[
Menu="Utilities"
Title="BuddyNet"
Icon="buddynet.png"
---
<?php
$cfg = parse_plugin_cfg("buddynet");
function v($cfg,$k,$d=''){ return htmlspecialchars(isset($cfg[$k])?$cfg[$k]:$d); }
$running = trim(shell_exec("/etc/rc.d/rc.buddynet status 2>/dev/null"));
$last = trim(shell_exec("grep -aE 'ONLINE|OFFLINE|REACHABLE|DISCONNECTED|waiting for buddy|tunnel error|^error' /var/log/buddynet.log 2>/dev/null | tail -n 1"));
// The node's identity comes from the binary, not from the log: `identity` reads
// the key file and creates NOTHING (only `init` does), so this is side-effect
// free — and unlike the log grep it still works on a fresh install and after a
// log rotation, which is exactly when pairing needs it. The grep stays as a
// fallback for the case where the array (and thus id.key) is not mounted yet.
$ident = trim(shell_exec("/usr/local/bin/buddynet --key /mnt/user/appdata/buddynet/id.key identity 2>/dev/null"));
if (!preg_match('#^[A-Za-z0-9+/]{43}=$#', $ident)) {
  $ident = trim(shell_exec("grep -aoE 'identity [A-Za-z0-9+/=]{40,}' /var/log/buddynet.log 2>/dev/null | tail -n 1 | cut -d' ' -f2"));
}
if (!preg_match('#^[A-Za-z0-9+/]{43}=$#', $ident)) $ident = '';
// The invite blob wants the key base64URL without padding (internal/invite),
// while `identity` prints standard base64. Convert once, here.
$identUrl = $ident ? rtrim(strtr($ident, '+/', '-_'), '=') : '';
// Our own virtual IP is a pure function of our identity key (identity IS
// address, see crypto/keys.go): 10.66.X.Y with X,Y = SHA-256(pubkey)[0:2],
// network/broadcast folded off. Needed for the SMB mount info.
$vip = '';
if ($ident) {
  $h = hash('sha256', base64_decode($ident), true);
  $x = ord($h[0]); $y = ord($h[1]);
  if ($x==0 && $y==0) $y = 1;
  if ($x==255 && $y==255) $y = 254;
  $vip = "10.66.$x.$y";
}
// Public shares are visible to ANY SMB client — including the buddy, the
// moment the tunnel is up. Surface them prominently (Unraid's default for a
// new share is Public). Missing shareSecurity means the default, i.e. Public.
$pubshares = array();
foreach ((array)glob("/boot/config/shares/*.cfg") as $f) {
  $c = (array)@parse_ini_file($f);
  $sec = strtolower($c['shareSecurity'] ?? 'public');
  $exp = strtolower($c['shareExport'] ?? 'e');
  if ($exp != '-' && $sec == 'public') $pubshares[] = basename($f, '.cfg');
}
// online state from the last meaningful log line
if    (strpos($last,'✓ ONLINE')!==false || strpos($last,'REACHABLE')!==false) $online='🟢 online';
elseif(strpos($last,'waiting')!==false)                                        $online='🟡 waiting for buddy';
elseif(strpos($last,'OFFLINE')!==false || strpos($last,'DISCONNECTED')!==false)$online='🔴 buddy offline';
else                                                                            $online=$last?:'unknown';
?>
<div style="margin-bottom:1em;color:#777">
The encrypted "cable" between two buddies — finds your partner via the handshake
server, punches through NAT (no port forwarding), runs a direct QUIC tunnel, and
falls back to a blind relay only if needed. Point rsync/borg/kopia at the local
socket.
</div>

<div style="margin-bottom:1em">
<b>Service:</b> <?=$running==='running'?'🟢 running':'⚪ '.htmlspecialchars($running?:'unknown')?>
&nbsp;|&nbsp; <b>Buddy:</b> <?=htmlspecialchars($online)?>
&nbsp; <a href="<?=htmlspecialchars($_SERVER['REQUEST_URI'])?>" title="reload status">↻</a>
<?php if($ident): ?>
<br><span style="color:#777">Your identity (give your buddy to pin):</span> <code style="font-size:.85em"><?=htmlspecialchars($ident)?></code>
<?php endif; ?>
</div>

<div style="margin-bottom:1.5em">
<?php foreach (array('start'=>'Start','stop'=>'Stop','restart'=>'Restart') as $a=>$label): ?>
<form method="POST" action="/update.php" target="progressFrame" style="display:inline-block;margin:0 .3em 0 0">
<input type="hidden" name="#command" value="/etc/rc.d/rc.buddynet">
<input type="hidden" name="#arg[1]" value="<?=$a?>">
<input type="submit" value="<?=$label?>"></form>
<?php endforeach; ?>
</div>

<script>
function bnShow(id,b){var e=document.getElementById(id);
  if(e.type==='password'){e.type='text';b.textContent='hide';}
  else{e.type='password';b.textContent='show';}}
// This node's identity, base64url — the half of the invite that says WHO will be
// at the rendezvous. Empty when the node has no key yet.
var BN_IDENT_URL = <?=json_encode($identUrl)?>;
// Mint an invite: a strong 384-bit rendezvous token (64 base64url chars, the
// same shape as the CLI's `buddynet gen-token`) plus this node's identity, in
// the bnet1.<token>.<key> shape internal/invite parses.
//
// Note which value goes where. OUR field gets the BARE TOKEN; the BLOB goes to
// the buddy. Pasting our own blob into our own field would pin our own key as
// the partner's, and the connection would be refused.
function bnInvite(id){
  if(!BN_IDENT_URL){
    alert("This node has no identity yet, so it cannot mint an invite.\n\nStart the service once to create it, then reload this page.");
    return;
  }
  var a=new Uint8Array(48);crypto.getRandomValues(a);
  var s=btoa(String.fromCharCode.apply(null,a)).replace(/\+/g,'-').replace(/\//g,'_').replace(/=+$/,'');
  var e=document.getElementById(id);e.value=s;e.type='text';
  var o=document.getElementById('bn_invite_blob');
  o.value='bnet1.'+s+'.'+BN_IDENT_URL;
  o.select();
}
// BuddyShare needs the WireGuard plane and :445 in the scope. Never silently:
// SMB access needs the WireGuard plane and :445 in the scope. Never silently:
// this ADJUSTS THE VISIBLE FIELDS so the user sees exactly what will apply.
function bnShare(sel){
  if(sel.value!=='enable') return;
  var f=sel.form;
  f.WIREGUARD.value='enable';
  var e=f.EXPOSE;
  if(!/(^|[,\s])(tcp\/)?445($|[,\s])/.test(e.value)) e.value=e.value?e.value+',445':'445';
}
</script>

<form markdown="1" method="POST" action="/update.php" target="progressFrame">
<input type="hidden" name="#file" value="buddynet/buddynet.cfg">
<input type="hidden" name="#command" value="/etc/rc.d/rc.buddynet">
<input type="hidden" name="#arg[1]" value="restart">

Service:
: <select name="SERVICE">
  <option value="enable"  <?=v($cfg,'SERVICE')=='enable'?'selected':''?>>Enabled</option>
  <option value="disable" <?=v($cfg,'SERVICE')=='disable'?'selected':''?>>Disabled</option>
  </select>

Mode — how the two of you find each other. **Coordinator** is the usual setup and needs nothing from your router. **Direct** needs no server at all, but one of you must be reachable from the internet (a port forward and a DynDNS name):
: <select name="MODE">
  <option value="coordinator" <?=v($cfg,'MODE')=='direct'?'':'selected'?>>Coordinator — a handshake server introduces you</option>
  <option value="direct"      <?=v($cfg,'MODE')=='direct'?'selected':''?>>Direct — no server; you configure your buddy's address</option>
  </select>

Handshake server (host:port) — *coordinator mode*:
: <input type="text" name="SERVER" value="<?=v($cfg,'SERVER')?>" placeholder="vps.example:51820">

Server key (public pin — must match the server's identity) — *coordinator mode*:
: <input type="text" name="SERVER_KEY" value="<?=v($cfg,'SERVER_KEY')?>" placeholder="server Ed25519 public key, base64">

Invite (`bnet1.…`) — paste the one your buddy sent you, or click "create invite" to start the pairing yourself. For testing; prefer the file below:
: <input type="password" id="bn_tok" name="TOKEN" value="<?=v($cfg,'TOKEN')?>"> <button type="button" onclick="bnShow('bn_tok',this)" style="align-self:flex-start;flex:0 0 auto;display:inline-block;width:auto;min-width:0;padding:1px 10px;margin-top:.2em">show</button> <button type="button" onclick="bnInvite('bn_tok')" style="align-self:flex-start;flex:0 0 auto;display:inline-block;width:auto;min-width:0;padding:1px 10px;margin-top:.2em">create invite</button>

Your invite to send your buddy (filled in by "create invite" — it carries your identity, so it pins you on their side):
: <input type="text" id="bn_invite_blob" readonly placeholder="click &quot;create invite&quot; above — then send this whole string to your buddy" style="font-family:monospace">

Invite file (on the array — overrides the field above, recommended):
: <input type="text" name="TOKEN_FILE" value="<?=v($cfg,'TOKEN_FILE')?>" placeholder="/mnt/user/appdata/buddynet/token — holds the bnet1.… invite (chmod 0600)">

Buddy key (public pin — your buddy's identity). In coordinator mode this is **only needed for a bare legacy token**, since a `bnet1.…` invite pins by itself. **In direct mode it is required**: with no server there is nothing else that authenticates your buddy:
: <input type="text" name="PEER_KEY" value="<?=v($cfg,'PEER_KEY')?>" placeholder="buddy Ed25519 identity, base64">

Buddy endpoint — *direct mode*. Where your buddy listens, `host:port`. A DynDNS name is fine: it is re-resolved on every attempt, so a changing address needs no edit here. Leave empty if your buddy dials YOU:
: <input type="text" name="PEER_ENDPOINT" value="<?=v($cfg,'PEER_ENDPOINT')?>" placeholder="buddy.duckdns.org:51820">

Listen port — *direct mode*. A fixed UDP port so your buddy can dial this server. **Forward this port to this machine in your router**, and leave it empty if you only dial out:
: <input type="text" name="LISTEN_PORT" value="<?=v($cfg,'LISTEN_PORT')?>" placeholder="51820">

Expose local port (-L) — to push backups TO your buddy:
: <input type="text" name="LISTEN" value="<?=v($cfg,'LISTEN')?>" placeholder="127.0.0.1:9000 (leave empty to disable)">

Forward to local service (-forward) — to receive backups FROM your buddy:
: <input type="text" name="FORWARD" value="<?=v($cfg,'FORWARD')?>" placeholder="127.0.0.1:873 (leave empty to disable)">

BuddyDNS name (--name) — reachable as &lt;name&gt;.buddy:
: <input type="text" name="NAME" value="<?=v($cfg,'NAME')?>" placeholder="alice (letters/digits/hyphens, max 63 — leave empty to disable)">

BuddyDNS resolver (--dns) — answer *.buddy on 127.0.0.153:53:
: <select name="DNS">
  <option value="disable" <?=v($cfg,'DNS')=='disable'?'selected':''?>>Disabled</option>
  <option value="enable"  <?=v($cfg,'DNS')=='enable'?'selected':''?>>Enabled</option>
  </select>

Lazy tunnel (--lazy) — defer the tunnel until the first -L connection (needs -L):
: <select name="LAZY">
  <option value="disable" <?=v($cfg,'LAZY')=='disable'?'selected':''?>>Disabled</option>
  <option value="enable"  <?=v($cfg,'LAZY')=='enable'?'selected':''?>>Enabled</option>
  </select>


WireGuard data plane (--wireguard) — carry the peer tunnel over kernel WireGuard instead of QUIC; the partner is reachable natively at its VIP (10.66.X.Y). Needs your buddy's identity pinned — from a `bnet1.…` invite or the Buddy key field above:
: <select name="WIREGUARD">
  <option value="disable" <?=v($cfg,'WIREGUARD')=='disable'?'selected':''?>>Disabled</option>
  <option value="enable"  <?=v($cfg,'WIREGUARD')=='enable'?'selected':''?>>Enabled</option>
  </select>

Exposed ports (--expose, WireGuard only) — the port(s) your buddy may reach on THIS server over the tunnel, e.g. "873" or "873,8080" or "tcp/873,udp/51820"; "all" = explicit whole-host access. **Empty = NOTHING is exposed (fail-closed, secure default)** — your buddy cannot reach any service here until you name a port:
: <input type="text" name="EXPOSE" value="<?=v($cfg,'EXPOSE')?>" placeholder="empty = nothing exposed (secure default)">

_(SMB access for your buddy — expose Samba over the tunnel)_ (_(needs WireGuard; selecting Enabled fills in WireGuard + port 445 above, visibly)_):
: <select name="BUDDYSHARE" onchange="bnShare(this)">
  <option value="disable" <?=v($cfg,'BUDDYSHARE')=='disable'?'selected':''?>>_(Disabled)_</option>
  <option value="enable"  <?=v($cfg,'BUDDYSHARE')=='enable'?'selected':''?>>_(Enabled)_</option>
  </select>

_(Share user for your buddy)_ (_(an Unraid user YOU create under Users — this plugin never creates or changes users)_):
: <input type="text" name="SHARE_USER" maxlength="30" value="<?=v($cfg,'SHARE_USER')?>" placeholder="bob">

&nbsp;
: <input type="submit" value="Apply">

</form>

Set **both** addresses for back-and-forth backup over one tunnel: -L lets you
rsync to your buddy (`rsync://localhost:9000/...`), -forward lets your buddy
reach your local rsync daemon on :873. At least one is required.

**BuddyDNS** lets you reach your buddy by a name instead of a virtual IP. Set a
**name** (e.g. `alice` → your buddy reaches you as `alice.buddy`) and/or enable
the **resolver** so this host answers `*.buddy` queries on `127.0.0.153:53`. To
use the names on the Unraid host itself, route the `.buddy` TLD to that resolver
(see the project [OPERATIONS.md](https://github.com/TZERO78/buddynet/blob/main/docs/OPERATIONS.md)).
**Lazy tunnel** keeps the -L listener open but defers the encrypted tunnel until
the first connection actually arrives (needs -L set) — handy when a backup tool
connects only occasionally.

####First contact — four steps, no terminal on either side

1. **You:** click *create invite* above and **Apply**. Send the whole `bnet1.…`
   string to your buddy over a channel you both trust (a call, Signal).
2. **Your buddy:** pastes it into their *Invite* field and applies. The invite
   carries your identity, so their side pins **you** automatically — they type
   nothing into their Buddy key field.
3. **Your buddy:** copies their own identity (shown at the top of their BuddyNet
   page) and sends it back to you.
4. **You:** paste that into *Buddy key* above and apply. Both sides are now
   pinned and the tunnel comes up.

Between step 1 and step 4 the log says the Buddy key is missing and the
connection is refused. That is the expected in-between state, not a fault: your
buddy pinned you in step 2, and you pin them in step 4.

Send your buddy the invite, and never paste your own invite back into your own
Invite field: it carries *your* identity, so this node would pin **itself** as
the partner and refuse every connection with `partner identity MISMATCH`. Your
own field holds the bare token — "create invite" puts it there for you.

This node runs the **buddy** role. Its identity key, trust store and peer cache
live on the array under `/mnt/user/appdata/buddynet/`. Because Unraid runs the
buddy headless, there is no terminal to compare the first-contact **safety check
(SAS)** — so your buddy's identity has to be pinned up front. There are two ways
to get that pin, and you need exactly one of them:

* **A `bnet1.…` invite** in the Invite field (or invite file) — it carries your
  buddy's identity, so BuddyNet pins it straight from the invite. Nothing to
  enter in the Buddy key field. This is the recommended way.
* **A bare token** (the older format, no `bnet1.` prefix) carries no identity. In
  that case you **must** fill in the Buddy key field with your buddy's identity
  (each node prints its identity at startup; for the server key, run
  `buddynet --role=handshake --key … identity` on the server).

Either way an unknown key is refused, never trusted blindly: a bare token with no
Buddy key set will not connect. See the project README and SECURITY.md.

**Secret note:** the USB flash is FAT, so file permissions are not enforced there
— an invite typed into the field above is effectively readable. For real
protection put the invite in a file on the array (e.g.
`/mnt/user/appdata/buddynet/token`, `chmod 0600`), set "Invite file" above and
leave the Invite field empty. The service then starts on array start, once that
file is available.

---
####SMB access for your buddy

_(With this enabled your buddy reaches exactly ONE thing on this server, Samba on port 445, and inside Samba only the shares you grant to the share user. Everything else stays invisible, fail-closed. BuddyNet only carries the connection — the shares, users and rights are Unraid's own.)_
_(Folder rights are plain Unraid: set the share to Secure or Private and grant the share user Read-only or Read-Write under Shares.)_

<?php if (count($pubshares)): ?>
<div style="border:1px solid #c00;border-radius:4px;padding:.6em .8em;margin:.8em 0">
<b>⚠ _(Public shares are visible to your buddy)_</b><br>
_(These shares are set to Public, so ANY SMB client — including your buddy once the tunnel is up — can open them)_:
<b><?=htmlspecialchars(implode(', ',$pubshares))?></b><br>
_(If that is not intended, set them to Secure or Private under Shares. New Unraid shares are Public by default.)_
</div>
<?php endif; ?>

**_(Setup — three steps, all with Unraid's own tools)_**
_(1. Create the user under Users — Add User; this plugin never creates or changes users.)_
_(2. Grant that user access per share under Shares — SMB Security, Secure or Private.)_
_(3. Send the SMB password to your buddy over a channel you trust. The password only selects the Unraid user; the security boundary is the pinned, encrypted tunnel itself.)_

<?php $su = v($cfg,'SHARE_USER');
if ($su) {
  $exists = preg_match('/^'.preg_quote($su,'/').':/m', (string)@file_get_contents('/etc/passwd'));
  echo '<p>'.($exists
    ? '✓ '._('Unraid user').' <b>'.htmlspecialchars($su).'</b> '._('exists')
    : '✗ '._('Unraid user').' <b>'.htmlspecialchars($su).'</b> '._('does not exist yet — create it under Users')).'</p>';
}
if ($vip && $su): ?>
<div style="border:1px solid #888;border-radius:4px;padding:.6em .8em;margin:.8em 0">
<b>_(What your buddy enters on the other side)_</b><br>
_(Server)_: <code>\\<?=$vip?>\&lt;share&gt;</code> &nbsp; _(User)_: <code><?=htmlspecialchars($su)?></code><br>
_(On Unraid use the Unassigned Devices plugin — Add Remote SMB Share. Prefer a soft mount: a dropped tunnel then gives errors instead of a hanging WebGUI.)_
</div>
<?php endif; ?>

---
####Danger zone
